Fixture input/output scan
Base revision: 58fbbd1119cc63e9f73b3fe0a5c40c7a52c37b44
Method: read each producer and its consumers sequentially. These are static
observations, not claims that a clean build or complete dependency closure passes.
Intended build driver: CMake generating Ninja; no implementation during this scan.
Quarantine decisions: docs/fixture-quarantine.log. Listed fixtures are excluded
from the new graph pending repair; inventory is not admission.
No fixture generation or runtime test execution is part of this scan. Shared tool inputs
are called out separately from fixture outputs; unresolved closure is explicit.

Shared exporter/auditor inputs (where consumed):
  bin/build-compiler.sh, bin/export-core.sh, bin/toolchain.sh, bin/plugin.py;
  selected GHC9.14.1/ghc-pkg/Cabal, THC plugin/shared library, registrations and
  package interfaces/libraries. Component builds also consume thc.cabal,
  cabal.project, Setup.hs, component sources and resolved plans/package databases;
  that closure is not individually enumerated in this scan.
  CBD inspection uses thc-compact, bin/core_package_manifest.py and
  bin/core_original_foreign.py. Audits consume bin/audit-core.py, imported core_*
  modules, bin/core-capabilities.json, scalar-primop-signatures.json,
  src/test/resources/thc/polyglot-abi.json and bin/simd-families.json.
  GHC/GHC_PKG/CABAL/THC_COMPACT and inherited tool/configuration environments select
  these tools. Export flags and pre/post stage options are separate inputs.

002  empty join semantics (fixture-free)
Owners: thc.runtime.TupleJoinLoweringTest and thc.runtime.EmptyArgumentRuntimeTest.
Inputs are typed Core modules or local frames built in the test; outputs are
observed values, ordered effects, failed-transfer state and released loans.
No producer, generated oracle or pre/post CBD products are required.
The source-core RepresentationAudit/RealCoreJoinTest owner (118) retains the real
recursive empty-tuple/scalar swap. Linux issue1066 remains open; this consolidation
does not establish a runtime fix or post-Tidy equivalence.
bin/test-empty-join-inputs.py independently tests auditor policy using constructed
Core and consumes no generated fixture products.

003  signed-narrow-primops
Fixture rationale (003 signed-narrow-primops)
Purpose: Check sign extension, narrowing and signed boundary arithmetic against GHC.
Produces/consumed result: Exported primitive CBDs and native oracle.tsv.
Cost and overlap: Keep one bounded signed-width corpus; array fixtures own exported
  literal transport, and fixture-free narrow carrier controls own loader behavior.
Build status: Value review only; admission still requires explicit inputs and single-
  owner outputs.

Producer: t/haskell-fixtures/Main.hs:prepare root SignedNarrow, invoked as
  cabal run exe:thc-fixtures --offline -- signed-narrow.
Consumer: src/test/java/thc/SignedNarrowPrimopsTest.java.

Files/tools in:
  t/fixtures/compiler/SignedNarrowPrimopsAudit.hs
  t/haskell-fixtures/Main.hs (entry list, operand cases, generated native driver)
  t/haskell-fixtures/FixtureSupport.hs (subprocesses, platform adapter, hashing)
  Built exe:thc-fixtures and its Cabal component/package closure.
  Shared exporter/plugin/GHC package inputs. No Python auditor stage.
  Windows: bin/export-core.ps1 and bin/windows-common.ps1; PowerShell, generated
    GHC response file; native executable has .exe suffix.
  GHC selects native compiler; exporter additionally selects GHC_PKG/CABAL and
    THC_SOURCE_NOTES. Inherited environment/package configuration remains input.

Artifact-by-artifact flow under build/signed-narrow-primops/:
  core/SignedNarrowPrimopsAudit.cbd and core/THC.InterfaceClosure.cbd
    IN: SignedNarrowPrimopsAudit.hs, shared plugin/package interfaces and exporter;
        named closure roots from Main.hs (48 wrappers plus signedNarrowDispatch).
    OUT: both CBD files; core-ghc/ GHC interfaces/objects/Windows export.args.
  NativeSignedNarrowPrimops.hs
    IN: Main.hs oracleDriver, entries SignedNarrow and module name.
    OUT: generated native oracle driver source importing SignedNarrowPrimopsAudit.
  native/signed-narrow-primops-oracle[.exe]
    IN: generated driver, SignedNarrowPrimopsAudit.hs, selected GHC packages and
        native tools; --make -O2 -fforce-recomp -dcore-lint -dstg-lint.
    OUT: native executable and native/ .hi/.o intermediates.
  oracle.tsv
    IN: native executable; stdin request rows computed from Main.hs operands.
    OUT: four-column native results after checking exact requested row keys.
  manifest.json
    IN: two CBDs, generated driver, oracle.tsv, native executable; hashes of
        inputPaths (fixture source, thc.cabal, Main.hs, FixtureSupport.hs, shell/
        PowerShell exporter files, toolchain.sh, plugin.py, top-level compiler hs).
    OUT: entries, module paths, composite name, row count, exclusions, hashes.

Actual test reads/writes:
  Reads manifest.json, all inputHashes/artifactHashes paths, both CBDs and TSV.
  Independent ScalarPrimopModel checks native results. NumericPrimopCoreEvidence
    reads decoded modules; CoreCbdFixtures reads CBDs using the Java loader.
  Direct canonical-result and wrong-arity controls also read entries from manifest.
    They write temporary synthetic CBDs through CoreCbdFixtures.write; that adds
    the shared CBD encoder (exe:thc-fixtures), even beyond preparation itself.
  Runtime/test classpath and helper implementations are separate test inputs.

Missing edges / caveats:
  Source receipts enumerate only top-level src/compiler/THC/*.hs, not recursive
    compiler modules or Cabal's full dependency closure. They are not build edges.
  Exporter builds its own plugin and compact tool. Runtime-generated synthetic
    CBDs need an explicit encoder dependency, not a previously built sidecar.
  All family outputs are produced in order by one function; the manifest is
    removed first, but old CBD files remain. Existence checks alone cannot prove
    that this invocation produced both modules.
  No other fixture directory is read by this producer.

