Shared producers and entry points (outside individual group numbering)

Directory state is a defect, not a prerequisite to preserve
  An empty output directory is not a file input. Do not translate that requirement
  into an order-only "clean" step. A rule must consume named inputs and own named
  outputs; unrelated files must not affect those outputs. Private scratch state is
  different from a shared/published output directory.
  Concrete examples already found above:
  - 030-033 SIMD bytearray: previous receipt and all old artifacts are copied before
    generation; a missing old artifact can prevent its own replacement. Random
    attempt directories also enter the new receipt. Remove the backwards edge.
  - 045 fetch-add-int-array and 081 mutvar: extra CBDs in the output directory fail
    exact-inventory checks. Consume the declared CBD pair, not a directory listing.
  - 088 aggregate/sum producers: recursive output scans incorporate stale compiler
    intermediates into receipts and therefore into consumer input sets.
  - 044 boxed-cas and 098 original-stack: directory state selects run-N output paths;
    previous receipts and attempt directories accumulate.
  - 119 corpus deletes group directories; 133
    managed-mvars refresh deletes its tree. Those observations identify current
    replacement policy; they do not justify a clean-directory graph dependency.
  - prepare-tests.sh removes build/synchronous-exceptions before its producer.
    This is another procedural workaround, not a source-to-output edge.
  Some filesystem behavior tests legitimately create/remove their own test files
  (unlink, directory operations). Their isolated test data is distinct from build
  output cleanup. Do not conflate the two when removing these requirements.
  Required incremental behavior: rerunning a producer over existing outputs works;
  deleting one owned output rebuilds it; adding an unrelated file changes nothing;
  failure cannot leave a fresh receipt claiming all outputs were completed.

Shared Haskell component builds
  Owner: Cabal, declared by thc.cabal, cabal.project and Setup.hs. cabal.project
  enables shared libraries and the development flag. Setup.hs uses defaultMain.
  IN selected GHC/ghc-pkg, Cabal/config/project-local settings if present, target,
  component flags, resolved package plan and package DBs, component source files
  and imported interfaces/native libraries. Cabal owns the transitive package
  build closure; fixture rules should depend on its products, not rebuild it in
  every export or invent a second package source graph.
  OUT dist-newstyle/cache/plan.json, component objects/interfaces/registrations,
  shared libraries and executables under Cabal's compiler/configuration paths.
  Resolve executable paths through the selected Cabal plan/list-bin, not guessed
  hashes or a list-bin invocation for a different compiler configuration.
